WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.658
One Rust library

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:02.543
replaces your whole

00:00:02.355 --> 00:00:03.203
VoIP stack

00:00:03.726 --> 00:00:04.480
rvoip is a

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:05.542
Rust library that

00:00:05.353 --> 00:00:07.334
bundles SIP RTP

00:00:07.405 --> 00:00:08.902
WebRTC and voice

00:00:08.773 --> 00:00:10.141
AI agents behind

00:00:09.952 --> 00:00:11.013
one call model

00:00:11.152 --> 00:00:11.942
There is no

00:00:11.754 --> 00:00:13.216
separate SIP proxy

00:00:13.157 --> 00:00:14.407
media server or

00:00:14.218 --> 00:00:15.256
RTP engine to

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:15.739
install

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:17.095
On a single

00:00:16.907 --> 00:00:17.755
cloud server

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:19.064
it sustained eight

00:00:18.876 --> 00:00:20.078
thousand three hundred

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.022
forty eight sessions

00:00:20.834 --> 00:00:21.635
per second of

00:00:21.506 --> 00:00:22.979
SIP signalling over

00:00:22.791 --> 00:00:23.663
TLS

00:00:24.182 --> 00:00:25.408
With encrypted media

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:25.856
flowing

00:00:26.057 --> 00:00:26.858
it still set

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:27.778
up eleven hundred

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:28.663
twenty four calls

00:00:28.474 --> 00:00:29.229
per second

00:00:29.441 --> 00:00:30.502
using the standard

00:00:30.338 --> 00:00:31.752
RFC benchmark with

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.049
zero failed calls

00:00:33.582 --> 00:00:34.596
Full story on

00:00:34.407 --> 00:00:35.303
the dashboard
